The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (group->domain != group->blocking_domain) {
    WARN_ON(__iommu_attach_device(group->domain, &pdev->dev, group->blocking_domain));
}
Reason:
When `group->domain` is NULL (e.g., because the default domain failed to allocate during first probe), the comparison `group->domain != group->blocking_domain` dereferences the NULL pointer, and the subsequent call to `__iommu_attach_device` also uses the NULL `group->domain` as the target domain, causing a crash.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (group->domain && group->domain != group->blocking_domain) {
    WARN_ON(__iommu_attach_device(group->domain, &pdev->dev, group->blocking_domain));
}
Reason:
The added NULL check (`group->domain`) ensures the re-attach is only attempted when `group->domain` is valid, preventing the NULL pointer dereference. If `group->domain` is NULL, the device remains safely parked in the blocking domain.

